WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.680
With "Spider-Man: No Way Home,"&nbsp;
the MCU is officially broken.
 &nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.480
"I am truly sorry for whatever I — and&nbsp;
everything I have ever done to you."
 &nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.840
Before we go any further, just&nbsp;
know there’s major spoilers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.840
ahead - not just for the end credit scene.
 
Thanks to Peter Parker and Doctor Strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.440
the multiverse splits wide open in the&nbsp;
third Tom Holland "Spider-Man" movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.120
In a lot of ways, this movie reaches back&nbsp;
to the prior Sony "Spider-Man" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.240
bringing in all kinds of characters and callbacks&nbsp;
that had nothing to do with the MCU – until now.
 &nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.160
But the biggest change for the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe happens in the movie's post-credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.000
scene, which acknowledges, for the first&nbsp;
time ever, an ongoing superhero series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.960
set outside the MCU.
 
"That guy, right?" 
 &nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.880
"Yep."
 
The first "Spider-Man: No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.440
Way Home" post-credits scene picks up around the&nbsp;
same time the events in New York come to a close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.760
with Doctor Strange casting a new spell to make&nbsp;
everyone forget Peter Parker again, no matter who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.000
they are and or what universe they're from.
 
The scene starts with Tom Hardy's Eddie Brock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.400
sitting at a tropical bar and talking to a&nbsp;
bartender about the events of the MCU so far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.840
This moment picks up from the post-credits&nbsp;
scene of "Venom: Let There Be Carnage,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.560
which sees Eddie and the symbiote wake up&nbsp;
in a new universe — apparently the MCU.
 &nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.800
He gets the general idea that Thanos used the&nbsp;
Infinity Stones to wipe out half of all life in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.040
the universe for five years before the Avengers&nbsp;
manage to bring everyone back in "Endgame." But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.720
because Eddie knows a little about&nbsp;
aliens, he starts to dispute the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.120
until Venom tells him he's drunk and starts to&nbsp;
force him to walk away from the conversation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.040
Before things can go any further, the&nbsp;
Lethal Protector is yanked out of the MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.960
thanks to Doctor Strange's second spell.
 
It's surprising the film doesn't keep Venom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.440
in the MCU. It would be a perfect opportunity for&nbsp;
Peter to go up against Eddie, but it does have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.240
one drastic effect on this universe. When Eddie is&nbsp;
zapped from the bar, he accidentally leaves a part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:47.520
of the symbiote behind. Uh-oh.
 
"Now dig on this."

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.080
The symbiote left behind in the MCU sets the&nbsp;
stage for Peter Parker to end up donning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.200
iconic black suit at some point in a future movie.&nbsp;
We already know Sony is supposedly planning a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.600
brand new trilogy with Tom Holland reprising&nbsp;
his role as the young wall-crawling hero. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.320
also know Holland's Peter still lives in the MCU&nbsp;
at the end of "Spider-Man: No Way Home." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.560
his circumstances are vastly different because&nbsp;
everyone has completely forgotten him thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.520
Doctor Strange's new spell. This allows Spidey&nbsp;
to go through almost a total reset in the MCU.
 &nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.560
After Spider-Man first encounters the symbiote&nbsp;
in the Marvel Comics issue "Secret Wars #8,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.600
he quickly gets enchanted by the enhanced strength&nbsp;
and speed it gives him. Now, it's not unreasonable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.840
to imagine that the same might happen to the&nbsp;
MCU's Spider-Man since he'll be operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.880
without most of his fancy nanotech and will not&nbsp;
have access to other Stark Industries resources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.920
As such, it'll be interesting to see how (or if)&nbsp;
the Symbiote Suit affects Peter – we know it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:47.360
do a number on the wearer's personality.
 
"You want forgiveness? Get religion."
 &nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.280
This event could still set up a future&nbsp;
confrontation between Eddie and Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.200
further down the line, too. In "Venom:&nbsp;
Let There Be Carnage," Venom starts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.360
tell Eddie that the Symbiote Collective&nbsp;
Mind knows plenty about other universes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.080
so it's not impossible they'll come looking for&nbsp;
the piece they left behind in the MCU. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.640
Sony and Marvel could choose to take things in a&nbsp;
completely different direction, but as of right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.080
now, the future of this universe is wide-open.
 
